# launch_daemon job for partition_grow. Installed as
# /boot/system/settings/launch/partition_grow, read by the *system*
# launch_daemon (see the note in launch-sshd about why the system context and
# not the user one -- there is no desktop on a Nitro instance).
#
# "job", not "service": this is one-shot. It grows the root GPT partition to
# fill a larger backing disk and then exits; there is nothing to keep resident.
# The program is idempotent -- on a disk the partition already spans it is a
# clean no-op -- so launch_daemon re-running it on a later boot costs one probe
# and changes nothing.
#
# It launches a wrapper (partition-grow.sh), NOT partition_grow directly. The
# wrapper defers the grow off the first-boot critical path: partition_grow's raw
# GPT rewrite makes the kernel rescan the boot device's partition table, and when
# that rescan lands in the first-boot service storm on a larger-than-baked root
# it races the network-gated launch jobs (sshd/EICE and the SSM agent), so on a
# big root neither ever came up (issue #254). The wrapper waits for the control
# plane to settle before doing the surgery. That is free: the partition grown
# here is only consumed by the *next* boot's root mount (the FS grow is owned by
# the BFS mount-time engine), so running it a little later changes nothing but
# the timing of the rescan. See partition-grow.sh for the full rationale.
#
# It still runs in its own job and therefore delays neither sshd nor the desktop;
# the wrapper's settle wait happens in this job alone.

job x-vnd.haiku-partition_grow {
	launch /bin/sh /boot/system/non-packaged/bin/partition-grow.sh
	legacy
}
